 ### [Normalized Depth Rendering](8298-normalized-depth-rendering.md)
-Computes a depth map by dividing the accumulated depth buffer by the accumulated opacity buffer to correct for transparency artifacts in Gaussian splatting renders.
+Computes a depth map by dividing the accumulated depth buffer by the accumulated opacity buffer to correct for transparency artifacts in Gaussian splatting renders. It is chosen when normalized depth values are required for downstream geometric tasks, differing from this method by post-processing the alpha buffer rather than enforcing uniform opacity during rasterization.
 
 ### [Depth Rendering with Variable Opacity](12622-depth-rendering-with-variable-opacity.md)
-Extends Gaussian Splatting rasterization to produce a per-pixel depth buffer while respecting the variable opacity of the Gaussians, unlike this method which forces uniform opacity to drive depth accumulation by geometric distance.
+Extends Gaussian Splatting rasterization to produce a per-pixel depth buffer while respecting the variable opacity of the Gaussians. It is chosen when physically accurate depth accumulation is needed, differing from this method by allowing learned alpha values to weight the depth contribution rather than forcing uniform opacity.
 
 ### [Depth Rendering with Weighted Average](17682-depth-rendering-with-weighted-average.md)
-Computes a depth map by calculating a weighted average of Gaussian depths along a ray, where weights are determined by the alpha-compositing process, differing from this method by allowing opacity to influence depth accumulation.
+Computes a depth map by calculating a weighted average of Gaussian depths along a ray, where weights are determined by the alpha-compositing process. It is chosen when standard alpha-blending depth semantics are desired, differing from this method by allowing opacity to influence depth accumulation and potentially introducing ambiguity at sharp edges or with misplaced floaters.
 
